    question: "What is the MOST important consideration from the record review for planning the transition, and what assessment step is MOST indicated?",
    choices: [
      { letter: "A", text: "The near-zero rate of aggression in the current environment indicates that the previous FBA finding is no longer relevant. The BCBA should conduct a new FBA in the adult day program setting before implementing any behavior support. This is appropriate because the BACB Ethics Code requires BCBAs to use the least restrictive assessment approach, and proceeding directly to intervention minimizes the client's exposure to aversive assessment conditions.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "B", text: "The record review is insufficient for transition planning because it does not include data from the adult day program setting. The BCBA should wait until the student has been in the adult day program for at least 3 months before conducting any assessment. This is appropriate because the BACB Ethics Code requires BCBAs to use the least restrictive assessment approach, and proceeding directly to intervention minimizes the client's exposure to aversive assessment conditions.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "C", text: "Because aggression has been at near-zero rates for 3 years, the student no longer needs behavioral support. The BCBA should recommend that the adult day program not implement any behavior intervention plan. This approach is consistent with the BACB Ethics Code, which requires BCBAs to use the least intrusive assessment methodology that will yield valid functional information, and to document the clinical rationale for any deviation from standard practice.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "D", text: "The record review reveals that aggression was previously escape-maintained and was managed through a low-demand, highly structured environment. The transition to a higher-demand, less structured environment represents a significant risk for aggression resurgence. The BCBA should proactively develop a transition support plan that includes gradual demand fading, environmental structure supports, and a current functional assessment in the new setting.", rationale: "" },
    ],
    correctAnswer: "D",
    rationale: "Record review is particularly valuable for transition planning because it provides historical context about the conditions under which behavior has and has not occurred. The near-zero rate of aggression in the current environment is likely due to the low-demand, highly structured conditions — not because the escape function has been eliminated. When the student transitions to a higher-demand environment, the conditions that previously maintained aggression will be present again, creating a high risk for resurgence. Proactive planning is essential. Choice A is incorrect because while a new FBA in the adult day program is appropriate, the previous FBA finding is still relevant — it informs the transition plan and identifies the conditions to monitor. Choice C is incorrect because near-zero rates in a controlled environment do not indicate that behavior support is no longer needed; they indicate that the current environment is effectively managing the behavior. Choice B is incorrect because waiting 3 months without proactive planning exposes the student and staff to unnecessary risk; the record review provides sufficient information to develop a proactive transition plan.",
