    scenario: "A BCBA is conducting a functional analysis (FA) for a 7-year-old boy with autism who engages in pica (eating non-food items). The BCBA is concerned about the safety of conducting a standard FA because allowing pica to occur during the assessment poses a significant health risk. The parents and medical team have requested that the BCBA identify the function of pica to guide intervention.",
    question: "What is the MOST appropriate approach to conducting a functional assessment for pica given the safety concerns?",
    choices: [
      { letter: "A", text: "Conduct a standard functional analysis with all four conditions (attention, escape, tangible, alone), but remove all non-food items from the environment during the assessment to prevent pica from occurring. This approach is consistent with evidence-based practice, as meta-analyses have shown that interventions implemented without formal FBAs are equally effective when the behavior topography clearly suggests the function.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "B", text: "Conduct the functional analysis in a hospital setting with medical staff present to manage any adverse events, as this is the only way to safely conduct a functional analysis for pica. This approach is supported by research showing that treatment response data is a reliable proxy for functional assessment outcomes when the behavior has a clear antecedent pattern across multiple settings.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "C", text: "Forgo the functional analysis entirely and rely on indirect assessment (interviews and record review) to identify the function of pica, as the safety concerns outweigh the clinical benefit of a functional analysis. This is appropriate because the BACB Ethics Code requires BCBAs to use the least restrictive assessment approach, and proceeding directly to intervention minimizes the client's exposure to aversive assessment conditions.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "D", text: "Use a precursor behavior approach — identify a behavior that reliably precedes pica (e.g., mouthing objects) and conduct the functional analysis using the precursor as the target behavior, which allows for functional assessment without the safety risks of allowing pica to occur.", rationale: "" },
    ],
    correctAnswer: "D",
    rationale: "The precursor behavior approach is a well-established method for conducting functional assessments when the target behavior is too dangerous, low-frequency, or otherwise difficult to assess directly. By identifying a behavior that reliably precedes pica (e.g., mouthing objects, picking up items) and using it as the target in the FA, the BCBA can identify the function without the safety risks of allowing pica to occur. Choice A is incorrect because removing non-food items from the environment would prevent pica from occurring but would also prevent the assessment from testing the conditions that maintain pica — the FA would not be valid. Choice C is incorrect because indirect assessment alone is insufficient for identifying the function of pica; a functional analysis provides more direct and reliable information that is essential for designing an effective intervention. Choice B is incorrect because hospital-based FA is one option but is not the only or most practical approach; the precursor behavior method is less resource-intensive and equally valid.",
