    domain: "F",
    scenario: "A BCBA is conducting a functional behavior assessment for a 9-year-old boy with autism who engages in stereotypic vocalizations (repetitive phrases from videos) throughout the school day. The behavior occurs at high rates during all activities, including preferred and non-preferred activities, structured and unstructured time, and when the boy is alone or with others. The teacher reports that the behavior does not appear to be triggered by any specific event and does not result in any consistent social consequence.",
    question: "What is the MOST appropriate functional hypothesis for stereotypic vocalizations based on this description, and what assessment step would MOST directly test this hypothesis?",
    choices: [
      { letter: "A", text: "The behavior is maintained by attention, because it occurs in the presence of others. A functional analysis with an attention condition would test this hypothesis. This approach is supported by research showing that treatment response data is a reliable proxy for functional assessment outcomes when the behavior has a clear antecedent pattern across multiple settings.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "B", text: "The behavior is a symptom of autism and does not have a behavioral function. No functional assessment is needed; the BCBA should implement a sensory diet to address the underlying sensory processing differences. This is the most efficient approach because conducting additional assessment when a clear hypothesis already exists wastes clinical resources and delays treatment, which is an ethical concern under the BACB Ethics Code.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "C", text: "The behavior is maintained by escape from demands, because it occurs during structured activities. A functional analysis with an escape condition would test this hypothesis. This approach is supported by research showing that treatment response data is a reliable proxy for functional assessment outcomes when the behavior has a clear antecedent pattern across multiple settings.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "D", text: "The behavior is maintained by automatic reinforcement (auditory/sensory stimulation), based on the high rate across all conditions, absence of consistent social antecedents or consequences, and occurrence when alone. A functional analysis with an 'alone' condition — comparing rates when the boy is alone versus when social consequences are available — would most directly test this hypothesis.", rationale: "" },
    ],
    correctAnswer: "D",
    rationale: "The pattern described — high rate across all conditions (preferred/non-preferred, structured/unstructured, alone/with others), no consistent social antecedents or consequences — is the hallmark of automatically reinforced behavior. The behavior appears to produce its own reinforcement through sensory stimulation (auditory feedback from the vocalizations). An alone condition in a functional analysis would directly test this by showing whether the behavior occurs at high rates even without social consequences. Choice A is incorrect because attention-maintained behavior would be expected to occur at higher rates in the presence of others and lower rates when alone — the described pattern of consistent rates across social conditions does not support attention. Choice C is incorrect because escape-maintained behavior would be expected to occur specifically during demand conditions and decrease during preferred activities — the described pattern of consistent rates across all activities does not support escape. Choice B is incorrect because all behavior has a function, and automatic reinforcement is a well-established behavioral function; dismissing the behavior as a symptom without functional assessment is inconsistent with evidence-based practice.",
