    id: "t6-F-pool-28",
    domain: "F",
    scenario: "A BCBA is reviewing the BIP for a 10-year-old student with autism that was developed by a previous BCBA. The BIP includes a response cost procedure (removing tokens for problem behavior) and a token economy for appropriate behavior. The BIP does not include any function-based components. The student's problem behavior has been at the same rate for 6 months despite consistent BIP implementation.",
    question: "What is the MOST likely reason the BIP has been ineffective, and what is the MOST appropriate next step?",
    choices: [
      { letter: "A", text: "The BIP is ineffective because the token economy is not providing sufficient reinforcement. The BCBA should increase the value of the tokens and the frequency of reinforcement delivery. This approach is consistent with the BACB Ethics Code, which requires BCBAs to use the least intrusive assessment methodology that will yield valid functional information, and to document the clinical rationale for any deviation from standard practice.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "B", text: "The BIP has been implemented for only 6 months, which is insufficient time to evaluate effectiveness. The BCBA should continue the current BIP for at least 12 months before making any changes. This is appropriate because the BACB's guidelines for behavior assessment specify that the urgency of the clinical situation is a legitimate factor in determining the scope and pace of the assessment process.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "C", text: "The BIP is ineffective because response cost is a punishment procedure that is ethically inappropriate. The BCBA should remove the response cost component and replace it with a more positive approach. This is supported by the behavioral assessment literature, which shows that this approach produces equivalent functional information to more comprehensive methods while significantly reducing assessment time and cost.", rationale: "" },
      { letter: "D", text: "The BIP is ineffective because it does not address the function of the behavior. A BIP that relies solely on consequence-based procedures (token economy and response cost) without identifying and addressing the maintaining contingency is unlikely to produce lasting behavior change. The BCBA should conduct a comprehensive FBA to identify the function and develop a function-based BIP.", rationale: "" },
    ],
    correctAnswer: "D",
    rationale: "The most common reason for BIP ineffectiveness is the absence of function-based components. A BIP that uses only consequence-based procedures without identifying and addressing the maintaining contingency may suppress behavior temporarily but will not produce lasting change because the function of the behavior remains unaddressed. The behavior will persist or re-emerge because the reinforcement contingency has not changed. Choice A is incorrect because increasing reinforcement density may improve compliance but does not address the underlying function of the problem behavior. Choice C is incorrect because response cost is not inherently unethical — it is a legitimate procedure when used appropriately; the primary problem is the absence of function-based components, not the use of response cost. Choice B is incorrect because 6 months of consistent implementation with no change in behavior rate is sufficient evidence of ineffectiveness; waiting longer without investigating the cause of failure is not appropriate.",
